---
// Fig. for "Coding Theory": fast Reed–Solomon encoding via the NTT, and
// why proof systems and data-availability schemes both lean on
// codewords being spread far apart.
---
<svg viewBox="0 0 760 400" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="width:100%;height:auto;display:block;">
  <text x="380" y="26" text-anchor="middle" fill="#e9eef5" font-size="16" font-weight="800" font-family="-apple-system,Arial,sans-serif">where zero knowledge uses these codes</text>

  <rect x="40" y="46" width="680" height="42" rx="10" fill="#111c2e" stroke="#3ab7ff" stroke-width="1.5" />
  <text x="380" y="72" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8fdcff" font-size="13" font-weight="700" font-family="-apple-system,Arial,sans-serif">Why do proof systems and blockchains care about error-correcting codes?</text>

  <text x="380" y="122" text-anchor="middle" font-family="SF Mono, Fira Code, Consolas, monospace">
    <tspan fill="#8b96a8" font-size="11" font-weight="700">ENCODE&#160;&#160;&#160;</tspan><tspan fill="#e9eef5" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700">message (3,5) = polynomial 3+5x, padded with zeros to (3,5,0,0)</tspan>
  </text>

  <text x="380" y="154" text-anchor="middle" font-family="SF Mono, Fira Code, Consolas, monospace">
    <tspan fill="#8b96a8" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700">NTT&#160;&#160;&#160;</tspan><tspan fill="#e9eef5" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700">evaluate at the roots of unity 1, 5, 12, 8 in F(13) &#8594; </tspan><tspan fill="#5ddc8a" font-size="13" font-weight="700">(8, 2, 11, 4)</tspan>
  </text>

  <text x="380" y="186" text-anchor="middle" font-family="SF Mono, Fira Code, Consolas, monospace">
    <tspan fill="#8b96a8" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700">SIZES&#160;&#160;&#160;</tspan><tspan fill="#e9eef5" font-size="13" font-weight="700">k = 2, n = 4, rate 1/2 &#8594; the message is blown up 2&#215;</tspan>
  </text>

  <text x="380" y="218" text-anchor="middle" font-family="SF Mono, Fira Code, Consolas, monospace">
    <tspan fill="#8b96a8" font-size="11" font-weight="700">DISTANCE&#160;&#160;&#160;</tspan><tspan fill="#e9eef5" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700">d = n &#8722; k + 1 = 3, so codewords differ in &#8805; 3 of 4 places</tspan>
  </text>

  <text x="380" y="250" text-anchor="middle" font-family="SF Mono, Fira Code, Consolas, monospace">
    <tspan fill="#8b96a8" font-size="11" font-weight="700">IN PROOFS&#160;&#160;&#160;</tspan><tspan fill="#8fdcff" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700">a cheating prover's word is far from every codeword; a few checks catch it</tspan>
  </text>

  <text x="380" y="282" text-anchor="middle" font-family="SF Mono, Fira Code, Consolas, monospace">
    <tspan fill="#8b96a8" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700">IN DATA&#160;&#160;&#160;</tspan><tspan fill="#8fdcff" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700">extend data with Reed&#8211;Solomon; any k of the n pieces recover everything</tspan>
  </text>

  <line x1="40" y1="312" x2="720" y2="312" stroke="#223049" stroke-width="1" />
  <text x="380" y="336" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8fdcff" font-size="15" font-weight="800" font-family="-apple-system,Arial,sans-serif">CHEATING MEANS BEING FAR FROM EVERY CODEWORD</text>
  <text x="380" y="358" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8b96a8" font-size="12.5" font-family="-apple-system,Arial,sans-serif">A low-degree polynomial is a codeword &#8212; encoded fast with the NTT from Topic 23.</text>
</svg>
